WEBVTT

00:00:00.648 --> 00:00:28.936
<v Riley>Hey there, morning. It's Friday, so we're deep into Wildlife and Conservation. Today on the show: a Mount Shasta climb where three 20-year-olds leaned on Gemini and came down in the dark, Italian fire sensors that spotted a blaze in minutes but couldn't get crews to it, and a Bella Coola bear case where officers still can't say how many grizzlies were involved. Plus, the Deep Dive asks who's on the hook when a sensor sees the fire first. Stick around.

00:00:42.411 --> 00:01:01.915
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Friday, November 6th, 2026. Today's theme is Wild Spaces &amp; Outdoor Life. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:02.915 --> 00:01:33.769
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today's bush stories run from fire sensors to chatbot trail advice. Quick weather check: out at Horsefly Lake this morning there's light drizzle and around 4 degrees, with a high of 10 and a low of 3 today. Expect slight snow showers through the day, with a 35 percent chance of about 2 millimetres of precipitation. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 3 under overcast skies, Williams Lake around 6 with overcast, Quesnel around 5 with light drizzle, and Riske Creek around 2 with partly cloudy skies.

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:34.785
<v Riley>Over to the news.

00:01:36.185 --> 00:02:23.735
<v Riley>Plenty of smoke and water in this morning's reporting, with a few practical items from our own backyard. The provincial Outdoor Recreation Fund has opened its fourth funding cycle with $400,000 for about 50 community projects. The Outdoor Recreation Council of B.C. manages the fund, which supports registered charities, non-profit organizations and Indigenous governments working on trails, stewardship and access. Two Cariboo-Chilcotin groups have drawn from it before. The 108 ATV Club received $10,000 to secure engineered railway access, improve trail visibility and upgrade gravel connectors. The Williams Lake Powder Kings Snowmobile Club received $10,000 to repair and upgrade its Yank's Peak backcountry safety cabin.

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:28.081
<v Casey>Two grants, two clubs, and somebody still has to keep that cabin roofed.

00:02:29.832 --> 00:02:58.606
<v Casey>Quesnel firefighters were called to a property on Redstone Road, on Plywood Hill, at 10:15 p.m. Fire Chief Ron Richert says crews arrived to a fully involved mobile home and were on scene for about four and a half hours with multiple apparatus. No one was injured, and the cause is still under investigation. Richert says the department has been to that address numerous times, including two earlier structure fires. He believes the trailer had been in a fire years ago and was reconditioned for living.

00:02:59.056 --> 00:03:02.496
<v Riley>Three fires at one address is a pattern somebody should be tracking.

00:03:04.304 --> 00:03:42.926
<v Riley>On October 22nd, a Williams Lake screening of the documentary American Doctor sold 165 tickets at Paradise Cinemas. The film follows three U.S. physicians, Dr. Thaer Ahmad, Dr. Mark Perlmutter and Dr. Feroze Sidhwa, who volunteered in Gaza hospitals and later spoke out about what they saw. Organizers from Williams Lake for Humanity and the Williams Lake Film club raised $890 for two charities, according to organizer Umrah Imran. The Reuters figure cited in the coverage puts the death toll in Gaza at more than 74,000 since October 2023, according to Palestinian authorities.

00:03:43.375 --> 00:03:47.679
<v Casey>A packed room in a small town holds a different conversation than a headline does.

00:03:49.466 --> 00:04:23.192
<v Casey>The BC Centre for Long COVID, ME/CFS and Fibromyalgia treats about 8,500 patients in virtual group settings. Participants meet with a doctor, ask about their conditions and medication, and can receive prescriptions after a one-on-one assessment. A patient from Winlaw, in the Kootenays, who has fibromyalgia, says the groups help her manage her energy and pacing. The Health Ministry has capped patients and changed fees for virtual groups, and the article describes the clinic as "a shadow of what it used to be." For rural patients, a virtual group can be the only specialist contact within reach.

00:04:23.642 --> 00:04:27.290
<v Riley>A fee change leaves the clinic open and makes every group smaller.

00:04:28.927 --> 00:05:13.773
<v Riley>Closer to home, the BC Wildfire Service plans to burn woody debris at two sites this winter, at Mirage Lake and Watch Lake. The releases went out on Tuesday, November 3rd. The Mirage Lake project covers 261 hectares next to residences, 13 kilometres northwest of the District of 100 Mile House and six kilometres west of the 108 Mile Ranch on the Tatton McKinley Forest Service Road. Of that, 138 hectares of woody debris will be gathered together and burned. Mechanical raking and pile preparation begin within the next week. Ignition may begin as early as December and run until March 31st, 2027, depending on weather and favourable venting conditions.

00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:41.669
<v Casey>Forbes profiles nine innovators using AI for planetary monitoring. One example is a smouldering wildfire in Ruffano, Italy, which AI sensors pinpointed within minutes in July. Firefighters still struggled to reach the remote terrain. The systems from Anduril, Dryad Networks and OceanX demonstrated detection, forecasting and species classification during recent competitions and expeditions. The piece's own argument is that detecting a disaster does not guarantee protection.

00:05:42.119 --> 00:05:44.311
<v Riley>Minutes to spot it, hours to reach it.

00:05:45.967 --> 00:06:17.901
<v Riley>Three 20-year-olds climbing Mount Shasta in Northern California leaned on Google's Gemini to plan their trip, according to WIRED, which reprinted High Country News. They brought too little food and overestimated their fitness. They reached the summit at 7:30 pm and had to come down in the dark, and by morning they were lost on a rough section of the mountain, with one hiker's knee banged up. A U.S. Forest Service program manager, Nick Meyers, said they did try, but "I think they kind of turned their brains off."

00:06:19.472 --> 00:06:37.064
<v Casey>Europe's 2026 summer fire season brought major conflagrations across Portugal, Spain, France, Italy, Greece, Belgium and Turkey, with lives and property lost. Albania and Germany were also affected. The report says Ireland and the United Kingdom saw wildfires of a scale and complexity well beyond the historic norm.

00:06:37.514 --> 00:06:41.082
<v Riley>Ireland and the UK on that list is the line that stops me.

00:06:42.696 --> 00:06:59.896
<v Riley>San Bernardino County, California is deploying Seneca autonomous firefighting drones, each carrying a 500-gallon foam payload. The county fire protection district's goal is to stop 90 percent of fires before they grow larger than 10 square feet, Tom's Hardware reports.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:26.566
<v Casey>The World Meteorological Organization says the strong El Niño developing in the tropical Pacific is expected to peak in December and is nearly certain to persist until February 2027. Secretary-General Celeste Saulo said the Pacific is drawing on heat stored beneath the ocean surface, and that the ocean is the warmest on record. The El Niño was declared in early June. That same El Niño is already soaking parts of the U.S.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:56.320
<v Riley>Scientific American reports that Florida and the Four Corners region have been hammered by major rain events this week. Homes have been swamped, roads washed out, and a house floated down the San Juan River in Colorado before crashing into a bridge. In Pagosa Springs, a major fiber cable was cut by flooding and cell service went down with it. The meteorologist there, retired Air Force meteorologist Arleen Prochazka, could only reach the outside world by email.

00:07:56.769 --> 00:08:00.201
<v Casey>Lose the fibre and the phones go with it, in town or on a trail.

00:08:01.842 --> 00:08:39.944
<v Casey>The UN Environment Programme says adaptation money is not reaching vulnerable communities at the scale needed. Least-developed countries and small island states need an estimated $40 billion to $50 billion a year, roughly 3 to 4 percent of their gross domestic product. Support to least-developed countries fell 16 percent from 2022 to 2024. Low- and middle-income countries accounted for 99 percent of the 765 million people affected by floods and landslides between 2020 and 2025. COP31 opens in Antalya on November 9th, and campaigners want a concrete funding plan on the table.

00:08:41.474 --> 00:09:05.362
<v Riley>A UN-backed report from the International Cryosphere Climate Initiative says the world's frozen regions face irreversible damage as warming nears 1.5 degrees Celsius. Its chief science adviser, James Kirkham, said the world has lost the chance to avoid exceeding that threshold, though overshoot can still be limited. The report says there are no good outcomes in remaining above 1.5 degrees.

00:09:06.762 --> 00:09:36.904
<v Casey>Livestock guidance from the United States, France and Poland focuses on forage during drought. In France, nutrition consultant Eva Garre urged farmers to measure silage and hay reserves and account for losses of 15 to 30 percent. Early maize samples there showed starch levels of 18 to 20 points, against the usual 34 to 37 in her region. Kenny Simon, an extension forages instructor at the University of Arkansas, recommends rotational grazing and moving hay-feeding sites to reduce pressure on dry pasture.

00:09:37.354 --> 00:09:40.210
<v Riley>Measure the hay before the winter measures it for you.

00:09:41.862 --> 00:10:11.414
<v Riley>In September, Montana wildlife officials euthanized 14 black bears at a property near Bozeman. Investigators responded to a report of suspected illegal bear feeding, and on September 24th and 25th they found 15 bears that were severely food-conditioned and habituated to people. One cub went to Montana's Helena facility for possible zoo placement. The investigation is ongoing, and officials have not determined how long the feeding went on or what the bears were eating.

00:10:12.999 --> 00:10:34.191
<v Casey>A hobbyist's build guide puts a solar-powered Meshtastic node into a weatherproof case for an outdoor test. The author calls it a working build rather than a proven, self-sufficient solar installation, because the small panel may not keep the battery charged. The guide also notes that the board lacks the Wi-Fi capability of the Duo, which suits a node that only needs to relay messages.

00:10:34.641 --> 00:10:39.225
<v Riley>The author's own caveat about the panel is the most useful line in the guide.

00:10:41.058 --> 00:11:14.666
<v Riley>Sweden's Board of Agriculture reports that its valuable meadows and grazing lands have largely kept their natural and cultural value. Of 300,000 hectares identified as valuable, 260,000 have been fully inventoried. In 2020, about 175,000 hectares received environmental management payments. Repeat surveys 10 to 20 years apart found most sites were still meadows or pastures. Sites left unmanaged became overgrown, and the agency says continued inventories are needed.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:34.676
<v Casey>And a Nature study drew on 15 years of aircraft air samples over the Amazon. It suggests the rainforest may be shifting from a net sink, pulling carbon out of the atmosphere, to a net source. Satellites struggle to see through the cloud cover there, so researchers, led by Jen Johnson of the University of Kansas, went up in planes.

00:11:35.126 --> 00:11:42.566
<v Riley>That wraps the morning's stories. Next up, a Cariboo group that builds homes and keeps building materials out of the landfill.

00:11:43.966 --> 00:12:09.926
<v Riley>Habitat for Humanity Cariboo builds affordable homes for Cariboo families who need them. A lot of that work runs through its ReStore, which takes donated building materials that would otherwise end up in the landfill and sells them, with the proceeds going back into housing projects. So if you've pulled out a perfectly good door, or you're hunting for supplies for a renovation, the website is habitatcariboo.org.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:15.372
<v Casey>Lumber that stays out of the dump and ends up under somebody's roof. Short loop, good one.

00:12:16.272 --> 00:12:21.656
<v Riley>The sensors in those stories do the seeing. Somebody else has to do the acting, so we start there.

00:12:23.056 --> 00:12:39.504
<v Riley>When a sensor sees the fire or the bear first, who is on the hook for the next move, and does the tool change what people do out there? The Italian fire we mentioned up top is the case for sensors. The detection worked, fast. The crew still had trouble reaching the ground.

00:12:39.822 --> 00:12:43.414
<v Casey>The sensor worked. The terrain didn't. Nobody budgets for that gap.

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:58.760
<v Riley>The detection capability is real. The Forbes piece describes systems that forecast fire behaviour and classify species from sensor data, and that is a large step past a lookout tower. But a minute saved at the sensor helps the crew only if the access road is open.

00:12:59.276 --> 00:13:09.468
<v Casey>Minutes saved at the sensor, then hours lost on a rutted road. Who maintains that road, and who decides which fire gets the crew first when two sensors go off in the same afternoon?

00:13:09.733 --> 00:13:18.237
<v Riley>That's a dispatch problem, and it has an answer: a ranked queue, written down before the season starts. Detection is what makes the queue possible in the first place.

00:13:18.748 --> 00:13:25.100
<v Casey>Writing it down is easy. The queue is only as good as the crew that answers it, and crews are the line item that gets cut first.

00:13:25.763 --> 00:13:30.827
<v Riley>Then ask for the staffing number next to the sensor number. Public budgets carry both.

00:13:31.372 --> 00:13:47.332
<v Casey>San Bernardino is the same question with drones attached. Five hundred gallons of foam per drone, and that 10-square-foot goal. Nothing says confidence like a number that small. Who launches them, who keeps them flying in January, and what do they cost per year once the demo is over?

00:13:47.626 --> 00:14:06.994
<v Riley>The county hasn't released a purchase price or an annual fee for its five drones. The closest comparison is the Aspen Fire Protection District, which signed a $5 million multi-year agreement for a similar five-drone system. And drones skip the road, which is the whole pitch. The first hour is where most fires are won or lost.

00:14:07.662 --> 00:14:14.958
<v Casey>Five million dollars in Aspen, and nobody in San Bernardino will say their number. The maintenance contract is the line nobody announces.

00:14:15.577 --> 00:14:21.473
<v Riley>A published target is how a track record starts. The county can be held to the 90 percent.

00:14:22.126 --> 00:14:34.478
<v Casey>And Europe's summer is why the target matters. Seven countries with lives and property lost, and Ireland and the UK burning past their historic norm. A county plan has to be ready for a season that doesn't look like the last one.

00:14:35.106 --> 00:14:56.130
<v Riley>Mount Shasta is the failure on the other side. Three 20-year-olds had a planning tool and still summited at 7:30 pm with too little food. The Siskiyou County Sheriff's Office and Forest Service climbing rangers recommend a noon turnaround on that mountain. Seven and a half hours past it. Software can print a turnaround time at the trailhead.

00:14:56.738 --> 00:15:07.498
<v Casey>Printing it was never the issue. Nobody turned them around in the afternoon, and Meyers's point was that they stopped doing that job themselves. A chatbot can't see the sky from the rock face.

00:15:08.099 --> 00:15:16.163
<v Riley>Then build the check into the tool. A planning app that refuses to hand over a route until you enter a turnaround time is a design fix.

00:15:16.837 --> 00:15:23.637
<v Casey>And who keeps that check current across every trail? Snow changes a route in a week. The app doesn't know the snow is there.

00:15:24.275 --> 00:15:48.539
<v Riley>Which is why the off-grid layer matters. That Colorado town where a flood took out the fibre and the phones went with it is the argument for a Meshtastic node, which sends a short text over radio without a tower. The build guide shows the hobbyist version: a board, a battery, a small panel and a weatherproof case. For a crew on a backcountry road, that's the difference between a message and silence.

00:15:49.133 --> 00:15:55.005
<v Casey>The author himself doubts the panel keeps the battery charged. Off-grid kit works until the first cold week.

00:15:55.625 --> 00:16:03.793
<v Riley>So test it in a cold week and log the voltage. A dead node during a test teaches you something. A dead node during a search costs you.

00:16:03.972 --> 00:16:05.060
<v Casey>Then who reads the log?

00:16:05.363 --> 00:16:20.451
<v Riley>Montana gives the people side of the same problem. Fifteen severely food-conditioned bears did not gather overnight. A camera with a motion log on that Bozeman property might have caught the feeding in its early weeks, before the bears became a euthanasia case.

00:16:20.999 --> 00:16:30.351
<v Casey>Officials still don't know how long the feeding went on. A camera records what's in front of it. Somebody has to drive out and tell the people doing the feeding to stop.

00:16:30.882 --> 00:16:36.882
<v Riley>Enforcement is the gap, agreed. But a dated log lets an officer arrive with evidence instead of a rumour.

00:16:37.458 --> 00:16:42.938
<v Casey>A log nobody is assigned to read sits there. That person usually isn't on shift at two in the morning.

00:16:43.838 --> 00:17:15.628
<v Riley>Bella Coola is where detection stops being the issue and the response takes over. On B.C.'s Central Coast, in Nuxalk territory, Jennifer Balcarras, 48, and her daughter Selah Reed, 20, went hiking a short distance from home and were reported missing on a Sunday. Their remains were found about 150 metres apart, on Monday in the Snooka West Trail area and on Tuesday afternoon. A suspected grizzly attack. The first work was all about people: searchers out, the area secured, and the two of them brought home.

00:17:16.255 --> 00:17:35.007
<v Casey>And once that's done, the question shifts to the bear. Or bears. The Conservation Officer Service said more than one grizzly may have been involved. Insp. Jeff Tyre said it was too early to say whether this was the same bear from last year's attack on schoolchildren and teachers on a trail south of Highway 20. That bear was never found.

00:17:35.622 --> 00:18:00.126
<v Riley>As of the Wednesday update, no bears had been trapped, though there were multiple sightings. The service says any captured bear found to be involved will be dispatched under provincial human wildlife conflict response guidelines. The way officers establish involvement is forensic: capture, immobilize, take DNA, and match it against hair or other traces from the scene. Only a match leads to a kill.

00:18:00.639 --> 00:18:15.751
<v Casey>A DNA match is a better standard than a sighting. It still depends on what was collected at the scene, and on catching the right animal at all. Last year's bear walked away from every trap. If more than one grizzly was there, a match on one bear tells you nothing about the other.

00:18:16.409 --> 00:18:37.625
<v Riley>The province's count matters for proportion. CBC's figures show two fatal grizzly attacks in B.C. from 2012 to 2025, in 2017 and 2025, against 64 recorded grizzly attacks in the same period. Insp. Kevin Van Damme called last year's type of attack "extremely rare." A response has to be sized to that.

00:18:38.154 --> 00:18:56.010
<v Casey>Rare across the province. Not rare in that valley. Two grizzly attacks near Bella Coola in about a year, and one bear still unaccounted for. The Nuxalk Nation recommended residents be indoors by 8 p.m., unenforced, and RCMP and conservation officers began night patrols. Does an advisory nobody enforces change anything?

00:18:56.344 --> 00:19:08.520
<v Riley>People change behaviour when their own nation asks them to, and they can see the patrols. The cost falls on anyone who works nights or commutes, so the advisory should come with a stated end point.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:17.014
<v Casey>An end point tied to what? A trapped bear? A DNA match? If the answer is "when it feels safe," the valley lives inside the advisory indefinitely.

00:19:17.576 --> 00:19:33.832
<v Riley>The Nuxalk response already looks like the system I'd want everywhere. The nation issued the recommendation, and officers came in behind it. Shuttle services, fruit gleaning and attractant management are on the list, and all three cut encounters before anyone needs a trap.

00:19:34.496 --> 00:19:50.032
<v Casey>Gleaning the fruit is prevention that works. Whether the valley gets a say after a capture is still open. Who hears what the officers find, and who gets to question which bear is removed? Nuxalk members, local guides, trail users. They're the ones who walk those trails next spring.

00:19:50.685 --> 00:20:01.053
<v Riley>Then ask the service directly, in writing. Ask for the matching criteria before any removal, and ask for the findings when they exist. A valley can put that question today.

00:20:01.734 --> 00:20:12.494
<v Casey>And about advice. We know almost nothing about how the encounter happened. A mother and daughter on a short hike near home. Telling listeners what they should have done implies a fact nobody has.

00:20:13.016 --> 00:20:20.496
<v Riley>General readiness isn't blame. Bear spray and a clean camp help before any details arrive, and people are heading out regardless.

00:20:21.045 --> 00:20:35.189
<v Casey>A generic caution can quietly put the weight on the people who were out there. Keep the message about the animals and the agency, not the hikers. The Nuxalk Nation described this as retriggering last year's trauma. That's two families, and a valley, grieving.

00:20:35.789 --> 00:20:46.709
<v Riley>Here at home, the Mirage Lake burn we mentioned up top runs on the same prevention logic as the gleaning. Take the fuel away before the fire comes. Those piles sit right next to residences.

00:20:47.358 --> 00:20:55.350
<v Casey>And the weather decides the burn day. Who makes the call on venting conditions, and who tells the homes beside that pile before the smoke shows up?

00:20:55.664 --> 00:21:01.224
<v Riley>Ask the BC Wildfire Service for a named contact before ignition, and the notice plan in writing.

00:21:01.859 --> 00:21:08.715
<v Casey>For both projects, Mirage Lake and Watch Lake, and published. A named contact who only exists in a press release doesn't count.

00:21:09.615 --> 00:21:30.719
<v Riley>The Powder Kings' cabin at Yank's Peak is the other half of the same idea. A cabin is shelter, the oldest backcountry safety tool there is. It doesn't need a battery. It needs a roof, a stove that works, and someone who checks the door. Trail visibility, like the 108 ATV Club's grant paid for, tells a rider where the trail is before any sensor does.

00:21:31.295 --> 00:21:39.039
<v Casey>A cabin needs a roof every spring and an inspection after every big snow. The repair grant is the easy part. Who inspects it in year five?

00:21:39.364 --> 00:21:48.060
<v Riley>A snowmobile club can keep an inspection log. Signs work the same way. They're the cheapest layer, they work in every weather, and they don't need a network.

00:21:48.687 --> 00:21:55.159
<v Casey>Signs tell you where the trail is. They don't warn you about a bear at the bend. And somebody has to replace them after the plow.

00:21:55.759 --> 00:22:07.511
<v Riley>Sweden's meadow survey shows what sustained inventory looks like. The sites that kept their value were the ones somebody kept visiting. Upkeep is the difference between a meadow and a thicket.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:16.210
<v Casey>And the agency's own conclusion is that the inventories have to continue. The surveys only work while somebody keeps funding the people doing them.

00:22:16.829 --> 00:22:30.189
<v Riley>The Amazon study is that argument at continental scale. Fifteen years of people flying planes on schedule found a forest that may be turning from a sink into a source. Patient measurement caught a change no single snapshot would.

00:22:30.539 --> 00:22:34.467
<v Casey>Fifteen years of somebody showing up. That's the part that's hard to fund.

00:22:35.367 --> 00:22:46.551
<v Riley>The tools make a careful hiker more capable. Planning apps, mesh nodes and drones all give people better information, and the Nuxalk gleaning shows prevention works when a community owns it.

00:22:47.227 --> 00:22:57.659
<v Casey>The careless hiker is the one who needs the information and won't read it. And at Bella Coola, the best tool on the list, a DNA match, still depends on catching a bear that may not be the only one.

00:22:58.225 --> 00:23:08.369
<v Riley>Then the design target is the careless hiker, which is harder, and I'd still build for them. For Bella Coola, what could work is clear criteria, published before any removal.

00:23:08.988 --> 00:23:25.756
<v Casey>And what's still unproven is who decides the evidence is enough when officers can't say how many bears there were. The valley lives with that answer. If you're in Bella Coola or on a Cariboo trail and you have a view on that, or a cabin or sensor story, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:23:26.404 --> 00:23:31.004
<v Riley>Until then, log the voltage on your node and keep a noon turnaround in your pack.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:34.002
<v Casey>And think of those two families. Enjoy your weekend.

00:23:34.602 --> 00:23:53.674
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.